#--: Overview

The declarations themselves live in #~"lib/system/ast.z", which is the one
place they are written. Every definition, field, arm, generic parameter and
own-line parameter there carries its own doc comment, and #~"zls" surfaces
each on hover; the corpus runner ratchets that coverage under #~"make ci".
This page is the prose and the worked examples -- read it for how the unit
is meant to be used, and the source for the exact surface.

The #~"ast" unit exposes the compiler's parser and syntax-tree
types as a public API -- the analogue of Python's #~"ast" module.
#~"ast.parseSource" turns a source string into an #~"Ast" -- a
table of #~"astnode" rows plus a name pool -- entirely in memory; #~"ast.Parser" drives a full cross-unit parse
against a #~"zvfs.ZVfs".

#b:"Stability: preview." This surface ships alongside the #~"zl"
linter/formatter and may change. It is a thin re-export of the
compiler front-end (#~"lib/system/zparser.z", #~"lib/system/zast.z");
the type checker is #b:"not" exposed -- #~"parseSource" is purely
syntactic and does not resolve names or types.

Accessed via qualified names only -- #~"ast.parseSource ...",
#~"ast.Parser.create".


#--: Index

#p: #b:"Parsing"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=parsesource: #b:"ast.parseSource"] -- #~"function {source: String.take} out Ast"]
  [#*: [#> to=parser: #b:"Parser"] -- #~"class as { create: function out this  parse: function {:this vfs: zvfs.ZVfs rootid: zvfs.fsno mainUnitName: String.view} out Ast }"]

#p: #b:"Tree"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=node: #b:"astnode"] -- #~"variant { ...26 arms: program, unitdef, functiondef, objectdef, call, dottedpath, ifexpr, statement, error, ... }"]
  [#*: [#> to=node: #b:"unitrow"] -- #~"record { nodeid: nodeid  lineno: u32  colno: u32  kind: unitkind  body: kidspan  isGeneric: bool }" (the #~"unitdef" arm's payload)]
  [#*: [#> to=pos: #b:"nodepos"] -- #~"record { line: u32  col: u32 }"]
  [#*: [#> to=pos: #b:"posOf"] -- #~"function {n: astnode.view} out nodepos"]

#p: #b:"Names"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"Ast"] -- #~"class { nodes: (ListVal astnode i: nodeid)  names: StringPool  root: nodeid  ... }"]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"nodeid"] / [#> to=names: #b:"nameid"] -- #~"typedef u32" each]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"StringPool"] -- #~"class as { set  setOwned  find  get  eq  length }"]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"nameTextEq"] -- #~"function {ast: Ast.view ref: nameid s: StringView} out bool"]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"nameTextCopy"] -- #~"function {ast: Ast.view ref: nameid} out String"]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"nameTextAt"] -- #~"function {ast: Ast.view ref: nameid} outx String.view"]
  [#*: [#> to=names: #b:"poolTextAt"] -- #~"function {pool: StringPool.view ref: nameid} outx String.view"]

#p: #b:"Errors"

#ul:
  [#*: [#> to=errors: #b:"ErrorData"] -- #~"class { nodeid: nodeid  lineno: u32  colno: u32  width: u32  err: err  msg: String  ... }"]
  [#*: [#> to=errors: #b:"errorrow"] -- #~"record { ... err: err  msg: nameid  note: nameid  hint: nameid }" (the #~"error" arm's payload)]
  [#*: [#> to=errors: #b:"err"] -- #~"variant { ...one arm per error kind }"]
  [#*: [#> to=errors: #b:"errCode"] -- #~"function {c: err} out u32"]


#--: parsesource


Parse a single source string into an #~"Ast", in memory -- no
#~"ZVfs", no filesystem, no type checking. The #~"Ast" holds the
master node table plus the interned name pool; its #~"root" names
the #~"program" row, which wraps one parsed unit. A node is read
from the table by id with #~"tree.nodes.get i: id".

A #b:"syntax error does not fail" the call: the parser embeds an
#~"astnode.error" row at the point of failure and keeps going, so a
tool can report every error in one pass. Walk the tree and collect
the #~"error" arms to find them.

#code language=zerolang: """
  tree: ast.parseSource source: src.copy
  root: tree.nodes.get i: tree.root
  match root case program then print "parsed" else null
"""


#--: parser


The full parser. #~"parse" loads #~"mainUnitName" and every unit
it references from the #~"vfs" and returns an #~"Ast" whose
#~"program" root spans them all -- the entry point the compiler itself uses.
Use this (rather than #~"parseSource") when the input references
other units and cross-file positions must resolve.


#--: node

#~"astnode" is the syntax-tree #~"variant" -- 26 arms covering
definitions (#~"program", #~"unitdef", #~"functiondef",
#~"objectdef"), expressions (#~"call", #~"atomid",
#~"dottedpath", #~"atomstring", ...), control flow (#~"ifexpr",
#~"forexpr", #~"caseexpr", #~"withexpr", ...), statements
(#~"statement", #~"assignment", #~"reassignment", ...), and
#~"error". Every arm carries #~"lineno" / #~"colno". Name-bearing
operand arms (#~"atomid", #~"labelvalue") store their identifier as
a #b:"name pool id" ([#> to=names: see Names]), not as text. A row is
read from #~"Ast.nodes" by its #~"nodeid"; match on it to dispatch:

#code language=zerolang: """
  n: tree.nodes.get i: id
  match (
      n
  ) case call then {
      print "call with \{n.arguments.count} arguments"    # n.callable is the callee
  } case dottedpath then {
      print "path at line \{n.lineno}"    # n.parent / n.child are its two sides
  } case error then {
      print "syntax error at line \{n.lineno}"    # n.err is the kind, n.msg the text
  } else null
"""


#--: names

Two zero-overhead typedefs name the tree's id spaces, so one cannot
stand in for the other: #~"nodeid" (a #~"u32" over the single monotonic
node space -- a #~"nodeid" indexes #~"Ast.nodes" directly, and row
#~"0" is a sentinel placeholder meaning "no node") and #~"nameid" (a #~"u32" over the pool). Both compare
same-space only and read their sentinel with #~"isZero"; a container
never carries either, so a #~"(ListVal u32)" of child ids mints at the
read.


The #~"Ast" holds the tree's name pool. Identifier text is
#b:"interned": #~"atomid" / #~"labelvalue" arms hold a #~"nameid"
pool id in #~"name" (the id indexes the pool; #~"0" is a sentinel row
holding the empty text, so it reads as absent), and equal ids always
name the same identifier, so id compares replace text compares within
one tree. #~"nameTextEq" answers "is this node named X" without
allocating; #~"nameTextCopy" and #~"StringPool.get" return an owned copy
of the text (#~'""' for ref 0); #~"nameTextAt" / #~"poolTextAt" return a
BORROWED view of the text -- allocation-free, and the binding pins its
source for the scope.

#code language=zerolang: """
  tree: ast.parseSource source: src.copy
  n: tree.nodes.get i: id
  match (
      n
  ) case atomid then {
      if (ast.nameTextEq ast: tree ref: n.name s: "main") then {
          nm: ast.nameTextCopy ast: tree ref: n.name
          print "found \{nm}"
      }
  } else null
"""

Pool ids are #b:"per-parse": the pool hashes with a per-process
seed, so ids must never be persisted or compared across two parses
-- serialize the text instead.


#--: pos


#~"posOf" returns the source position of an #b:"operand-position"
node (the arms that appear inside expressions). Definition,
statement, and clause arms are not operand-position and return
#~"(0, 0)" -- read their #~"lineno" / #~"colno" fields directly
instead.


#--: errors


The payload of an #~"astnode.error" row is an #~"errorrow": its
#~"msg" (the human message), #~"note" and #~"hint" are pool ids,
read with #~"nameTextCopy" or #~"nameTextAt". #~"ErrorData" carries
the same failure with owned #~"String" texts. In both, #~"err" is
the classifying #~"variant" and #~"errCode" maps it to its numeric
#~"E####" code (parser/syntax errors are
#~"E0001".. #~"E0099"). #~"lineno" / #~"colno" / #~"width" locate
and underline the offending span.
